<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Wayland on Hadobot Blog</title><link>https://hadobot.github.io/tags/wayland/</link><description>Recent content in Wayland on Hadobot Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 03 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://hadobot.github.io/tags/wayland/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Unlocking Higher Display Resolutions on Wayland: A Step-by-Step Guide</title><link>https://hadobot.github.io/p/unlocking-higher-display-resolutions-on-wayland-a-step-by-step-guide/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://hadobot.github.io/p/unlocking-higher-display-resolutions-on-wayland-a-step-by-step-guide/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;If you’ve followed the previous article on using &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;xrandr&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; to unlock higher resolutions on Xorg-based systems, you might have wondered: &lt;em&gt;what about Wayland?&lt;/em&gt;
Wayland, the modern display server protocol replacing Xorg on most Linux distributions, handles displays quite differently. It’s faster, more secure, and avoids some legacy issues — but also removes tools like &lt;code&gt;xrandr&lt;/code&gt;, which means custom resolutions require a different approach.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this guide, we’ll explore how to achieve higher or custom resolutions on &lt;strong&gt;Wayland-based systems&lt;/strong&gt; using kernel parameters and EDID overrides. By the end, you’ll be able to force a resolution like &lt;strong&gt;1920x1080&lt;/strong&gt; even when your laptop or monitor doesn’t advertise it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-1-check-whether-youre-running-wayland-or-xorg"&gt;Step 1: Check Whether You’re Running Wayland or Xorg
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Before doing anything else, verify that your current session uses Wayland:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$XDG_SESSION_TYPE&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;If the output is &lt;code&gt;wayland&lt;/code&gt;, continue with this guide.
If it says &lt;code&gt;x11&lt;/code&gt;, your system is running Xorg, and you can follow the previous &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;xrandr&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; method instead.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-2-understand-the-difference-between-wayland-and-xorg"&gt;Step 2: Understand the Difference Between Wayland and Xorg
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Under &lt;strong&gt;Xorg&lt;/strong&gt;, users could freely add or remove display modes using &lt;code&gt;xrandr&lt;/code&gt;.
Wayland, however, delegates resolution management to the &lt;strong&gt;compositor&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., GNOME Shell, KDE Plasma, Sway). This design increases stability but means you &lt;strong&gt;cannot directly inject custom resolutions&lt;/strong&gt; at runtime.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Instead, you must:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Instruct the &lt;strong&gt;kernel&lt;/strong&gt; to pretend the display supports your desired resolution, or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide a &lt;strong&gt;custom EDID&lt;/strong&gt; (Extended Display Identification Data) file that includes your desired mode.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-3-determine-your-display-output-name"&gt;Step 3: Determine Your Display Output Name
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Each display output is represented under &lt;code&gt;/sys/class/drm&lt;/code&gt;. To find your active display:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;ls /sys/class/drm/*/status
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Then check which outputs are marked as &lt;code&gt;connected&lt;/code&gt;. For example, you might see:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;/sys/class/drm/card0-eDP-1/status: connected
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;In this case, your output name is &lt;strong&gt;eDP-1&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-4-force-a-resolution-using-a-kernel-parameter"&gt;Step 4: Force a Resolution Using a Kernel Parameter
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;If your monitor supports the new resolution but your system doesn’t expose it, the easiest method is adding a kernel parameter to force it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Open your boot configuration file (for example, on Ubuntu or Debian):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo nano /etc/default/grub
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Find the line that starts with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT=&amp;#34;quiet splash&amp;#34;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;and append your video mode, for example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT=&amp;#34;quiet splash video=eDP-1:1920x1080@60&amp;#34;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Update GRUB and reboot:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo update-grub
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo reboot
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After rebooting, open your display settings (GNOME Settings → Displays or KDE System Settings → Display &amp;amp; Monitor) and select &lt;strong&gt;1920x1080&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-5-using-a-custom-edid-advanced-method"&gt;Step 5: Using a Custom EDID (Advanced Method)
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;If the kernel parameter method fails — often the case with certain laptops or NVIDIA drivers — you can create a &lt;strong&gt;custom EDID file&lt;/strong&gt; to trick the system into recognizing the resolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Generate an EDID file using a tool such as &lt;code&gt;edid-decode&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;wxEDID&lt;/code&gt;, or start from a known-good one.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Save your custom file under:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo mkdir -p /lib/firmware/edid
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo cp 1920x1080.bin /lib/firmware/edid/
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Edit your GRUB configuration again and add:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;drm.edid_firmware=eDP-1:edid/1920x1080.bin
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Update GRUB and rebuild the initramfs (important for persistence):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;3
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo update-grub
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo update-initramfs -u
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo reboot
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After rebooting, check your available resolutions in your compositor’s display settings. The new resolution should now appear.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-6-select-the-new-resolution"&gt;Step 6: Select the New Resolution
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Once the kernel or compositor recognizes your forced mode, you can select it:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GNOME (Wayland):&lt;/strong&gt;
Open &lt;em&gt;Settings → Displays&lt;/em&gt; and choose the resolution manually.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;KDE Plasma (Wayland):&lt;/strong&gt;
Use the GUI under &lt;em&gt;System Settings → Display and Monitor&lt;/em&gt;, or use the CLI:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;kscreen-doctor --outputs
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;kscreen-doctor output.&amp;lt;ID&amp;gt;.mode.&amp;lt;MODE&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you don’t see your resolution, double-check the boot parameters and verify using:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;dmesg &lt;span class="p"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -i drm
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;to confirm the kernel applied the EDID or video mode correctly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="step-7-persisting-the-configuration"&gt;Step 7: Persisting the Configuration
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Unlike Xorg, Wayland doesn’t execute &lt;code&gt;~/.profile&lt;/code&gt; on login for display modes.
Persistence must come from:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GRUB configuration&lt;/strong&gt; (kernel parameters), or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;EDID injection&lt;/strong&gt; (firmware-level override).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once configured, your system will boot with the desired resolution every time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="quick-reference-wayland-edition"&gt;Quick Reference: Wayland Edition
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;
&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 3
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 4
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 5
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 6
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 7
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 8
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 9
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;10
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;11
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;12
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;13
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;14
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;15
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;16
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;17
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;18
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;19
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;20
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Check session type&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$XDG_SESSION_TYPE&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Identify active outputs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;ls /sys/class/drm/*/status
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Add a forced resolution via GRUB&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo nano /etc/default/grub
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Add inside quotes:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT=&amp;#34;quiet splash video=eDP-1:1920x1080@60&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo update-grub
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo reboot
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# (Optional) Use custom EDID&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo mkdir -p /lib/firmware/edid
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo cp 1920x1080.bin /lib/firmware/edid/
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Add in GRUB:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# drm.edid_firmware=eDP-1:edid/1920x1080.bin&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo update-initramfs -u
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;sudo reboot
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2 id="whats-next"&gt;What’s Next?
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;You’ve now learned how to unlock custom resolutions on both &lt;strong&gt;Xorg&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Wayland&lt;/strong&gt; systems.
While Wayland requires a bit more setup, these methods ensure your system boots with the exact resolution you want — no matter what display you’re using.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>